=== CORE THEOREM: Why tiling counts are always odd ===

THEOREM: For any tournament iso class C, #tilings(C) is ODD.

PROOF:
  (1) H(T) is always odd for tournaments. [OCF: H=1+2α₁+4α₂+... ≡ 1 mod 2]
  (2) Orbit-Stabilizer: #tilings(C) × |Aut(C)| = H(C)
  (3) H(C) is odd = product of two integers → BOTH must be odd.
  Therefore #tilings(C) is odd. ∎

COROLLARY: NS pairs contribute EVEN merged counts (odd + odd = even).
           SC classes contribute ODD individual counts.


=== EXPLORER CODE DECODED ===

TILING MODEL:
  • VERTS = [N, N-1, ..., 1]
  • Base Hamiltonian path: N→N-1→...→1 (fixed, always present)
    → A[k][k+1]=1 in adjacency matrix (0-indexed: index 0 = vertex N)
  • TILES: for y=1..N-2: for x=N downto y+2 → tile (x,y)
    → bit=0: A[xi][yi]=1, arc x→y  (x beats y, forward/off)
    → bit=1: A[yi][xi]=1, arc y→x  (y beats x, backward/on)
  • Arc (x,y): xi=N-x, yi=N-y in 0-indexed adjacency matrix

TILE ORDERING for n=5 (6 tiles, bits 0-5):
  bit 0: (5,1)  range=4, xi=0, yi=4
  bit 1: (4,1)  range=3, xi=1, yi=4
  bit 2: (3,1)  range=2, xi=2, yi=4
  bit 3: (5,2)  range=3, xi=0, yi=3
  bit 4: (4,2)  range=2, xi=1, yi=3  ← self-paired (4+2=N+1=6)
  bit 5: (5,3)  range=2, xi=0, yi=2

TRANS_MAP for n=5: [0, 3, 5, 1, 4, 2]
  Self-paired: bit 0 (5,1) since 5+1=6, bit 4 (4,2) since 4+2=6
  Paired: bits 1↔3 [tile (4,1)↔(5,2)], bits 2↔5 [tile (3,1)↔(5,3)]

GRID-SYMMETRIC: bits[1]=bits[3] AND bits[2]=bits[5]
  → 2^4 = 16 grid-sym tilings (2 free self-paired + 2 free pairs)

BLUE FLIP-LINE {T, T_flip}:
  T_flip = ALL tile bits inverted (tile complement)
  Blue = BOTH T and T_flip are grid-symmetric
  Black = BOTH are NOT grid-symmetric
  [Proof: T grid-sym ⟺ bits[1]=bits[3] ⟺ 1-bits[1]=1-bits[3] ⟺ T_flip grid-sym]


