=== ZECKENDORF CONNECTION ===

DEFINITION: Zeckendorf tiling = tiling with no two consecutive active tiles
  (no bits[k]=bits[k+1]=1 in the explorer ordering)

|Z_n| = F_{m+2} where m=C(n-1,2)  [pair automaton: DFA for no-consecutive-1s]

  n=3: |Z_n|=2, SC-Z=2, NS-Z=0, sum=2=2 ✓
         Zero-Zeckendorf classes: 0 (H=[])
         Max Z-count per class: 1 (H=1 class)
  n=4: |Z_n|=5, SC-Z=3, NS-Z=2, sum=5=5 ✓
         Zero-Zeckendorf classes: 0 (H=[])
         Max Z-count per class: 2 (H=5 class)
  n=5: |Z_n|=21, SC-Z=14, NS-Z=7, sum=21=21 ✓
         Zero-Zeckendorf classes: 1 (H=[15])
         Max Z-count per class: 4 (H=9 class)
  n=6: |Z_n|=144, SC-Z=33, NS-Z=111, sum=144=144 ✓
         Zero-Zeckendorf classes: 5 (H=[15, 33, 33, 41, 45])
         Max Z-count per class: 7 (H=29 class)

KEY ZECKENDORF INSIGHT #1: The Zeckendorf filter favors low-H classes.
  H=1 (transitive): 1 tiling, ALL Zeckendorf → Z-count=1
  H=max (most complex): few tilings but NONE Zeckendorf at n≥5 → Z-count=0
  Reason: high-H tilings need many backward arcs, which must be consecutive.

KEY ZECKENDORF INSIGHT #2: SC classes receive MORE Z-tilings than expected.
  n=5: SC classes have 8/12 classes but get 14/21=2/3 of all Z-tilings
  n=6: SC classes have 12/56 classes but get 33/144=23% of Z-tilings
        NS classes have 44/56 classes and get 111/144=77% of Z-tilings

KEY ZECKENDORF INSIGHT #3: The Z-count partition sums to F_{m+2}.
  n=5: [0,1,1,1,1,1,2,2,2,3,3,4] summing to 21=F_8
  n=6: [0×5,1×13,2×10,3×13,4×8,5×4,6×1,7×2] summing to 144=F_{12}

EXPLICIT: These are the sums of Z-count values:
  n=5: sum=21, max=4, #zeros=1
  n=6: sum=144, max=7, #zeros=5


