=== RECURSIVE STRUCTURE (as user described) ===

Each tournament = a ranking hierarchy:
  H=1: single ranking (fully transitive, 1 Hamiltonian path)
  H>1: multiple valid rankings, each corresponding to a different Hamiltonian path

The STAIRCASE structure (tile grid) captures this recursively:
  • Bottom row (y=1): arcs from vertex 1 to all others
     Determines where vertex 1 sits in the ranking relative to all others
  • Middle rows: successively shorter-range comparisons
  • The EXPLORER shows the recursive parent class (purple center label)
    = which n-1 class results from removing the top vertex (vertex N)

ZECKENDORF = canonical recursive decomposition:
  No two consecutive active tiles = a tournament where no two adjacent
  levels of the ranking hierarchy have simultaneous upsets.
  This gives the non-concentration property: upsets are spread out.
  Count: F_{m+2} = the Fibonacci number of valid spread-out upset patterns.

The sum 2+10+1+1+3+5+9+9+11+13:
  Writes 64 as sum of odd numbers (all tiling counts are odd)
  NS-pairs contribute even numbers (2=1+1, 10=5+5)
  SC classes contribute odd numbers (1,1,3,5,9,9,11,13)
  The pairing is by FULL arc complement (T^op), not tile complement
